# AtpClient — end-to-end demo

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:atp_client, "~> 0.6"}
])
```

`atp_client` exposes a single, normalized interface to four backends for
automated theorem provers. This notebook walks through each backend with a
runnable problem — no external servers to configure by hand for the
same-host paths.

Every backend takes an `AtpClient.Problem` — a binary, a `Tptp.File` or a
`Tptp.Unit` — and returns `{:ok, %AtpClient.Result{}}` (the system asserted
a verdict) or `{:error, reason}` (none could be obtained). The result's
`status` is the [SZS Ontology](https://szs.tptp.org) value as a
`Tptp.Szs.Ontology` atom (`:theorem`, `:unsatisfiable`, `:satisfiable`,
`:counter_satisfiable`, `:gave_up`, `:timeout`, …); its `output` is the
system's output verbatim, and `evidence` says how the status was read.

```elixir
alias AtpClient.{SystemOnTptp, Isabelle, LocalExec, StarExec}
```

<!-- livebook:{"branch_parent_index":0} -->

## SystemOnTPTP

The public `tptp.org` HTTP form. No configuration needed — the default URL
already points at `https://tptp.org/cgi-bin/SystemOnTPTPFormReply`.

```elixir
problem = ~S"""
fof(ax1, axiom, p).
fof(ax2, axiom, (p => q)).
fof(goal, conjecture, q).
"""
```

`list_provers/0` pulls the current system catalogue from the deployment
(cached in-process after the first call).

```elixir
SystemOnTptp.list_provers() |> Enum.take(10)
```

Run a single system, by name: `"Zipperpin"` resolves to the version
SystemOnTPTP currently advertises, which the result's `system` names. The
prover's stdout travels with the result as `output`.

```elixir
with {:ok, result} <- SystemOnTptp.query_system(problem, "Zipperpin", time_limit_sec: 5),
do: {result.system, result.status}
```

```elixir
with {:ok, result} <- SystemOnTptp.query_system(problem, "Zipperpin", time_limit_sec: 5),
do: IO.puts(result.output)
```

Fan out to several systems in one HTTP round-trip. Every system given
comes back, in the order given: a result, or the reason there is none — a
first-order-only solver handed a higher-order problem, a name the
deployment does not advertise.

```elixir
with {:ok, outcomes} <-
  SystemOnTptp.query_selected_systems(
    problem,
    ["cvc5", "Z3", "Nope"],
    time_limit_sec: 15
  ),
do: outcomes
```

`query_all_systems/2` uses `list_provers/0` and is convenient for
benchmarking, but it holds one HTTP connection open for the sum of the
per-system time limits — set `:time_limit_sec` conservatively.

<!-- livebook:{"branch_parent_index":0} -->

## Isabelle/HOL

`AtpClient.Isabelle` talks to an `isabelle server`. When `isabelle` is on
`$PATH` (or reachable via the `ISABELLE_TOOL` env var), the bundled
`:isabelle_elixir` package can spin one up for you — no manual server
management.

```elixir
{:ok, server} =
  IsabelleClient.start_server(
    server_name: "atp_client_demo_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"
  )

opts = [
  host: server.host,
  port: server.port,
  password: server.password
]
```

`:local_dir` defaults to a subdirectory of `System.tmp_dir!/0`, so the
same-host case (Isabelle running next to the BEAM) is zero-config. In
containers or Cygwin where the two see the same directory under different
paths, set `:local_dir` (BEAM view) and `:isabelle_dir` (server view)
explicitly.

Pass either a full theory or just a body — bare text is auto-wrapped in
`theory <name> imports Main begin ... end`. `prove_theory/4` returns the
same `%AtpClient.Result{}` every other backend does; Isabelle prints no SZS
lines, so the status is read from the server's messages and the result's
`evidence` is `{:backend, AtpClient.Isabelle}`.

```elixir
body = ~S"""
lemma "\<forall>x. P x \<longrightarrow> P x"
  by auto
"""

{:ok, session} = Isabelle.open_session(opts)
try do
  Isabelle.prove_theory(session, body, "Reflexivity")
after
  Isabelle.close_session(session)
end
```

For TPTP/THF problems, `query/2` routes through
`IsabelleClient.TPTP.isabellize_theory/1`, injects the configured
`:proof_method` (default `"by auto"`), and reads one status per conjecture
into `result.details.lemmas`. Names come from the input formulae.

```elixir
tptp_problem = ~S"""
thf(p_type, type, p: $i > $o).
thf(g_taut, conjecture, ! [X: $i]: (p @ X | ~ (p @ X))).
thf(g_false, conjecture, ! [X: $i]: (p @ X & ~ (p @ X))).
"""

with {:ok, result} <-
       Isabelle.query(
         tptp_problem,
         opts ++ [proof_method: "sledgehammer nitpick oops", use_theories_timeout_ms: 600_000]
       ),
     do: result.details.lemmas
```

`g_taut` should come back `:theorem` (sledgehammer found a proof),
`g_false` as `:counter_satisfiable` (nitpick found a counter-model).
The verdict comes from the tool's own message, so `oops` — which withdraws
the goal so Isabelle keeps checking the next lemma — is fine.

The session is what's expensive to start (~seconds for `HOL`); each
`prove_theory/4` call over an open one is fast. The block above already
demonstrates the pattern.

Shut the server down when you're done with it:

```elixir
IsabelleClient.Server.kill(server.name)
```

